        <p class="profile-detail-region">City · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Copán</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 900 BCE – c. 900 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">The first known stone architecture in the valley dates to about the 9th century BCE; the city was refounded in 426 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-yax-kuk-mo">K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'</a>, who established a new Maya kingdom and royal dynasty.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">In the alluvial basin of the Copán River, a fertile mountain valley among foothills in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, not far from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The dynasty of sixteen kings founded in 426; the Acropolis and its tombs; the Hieroglyphic Stairway with about 2,200 glyphs; Altar Q; the Rosalila temple; the carved stelae; the defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quirigua">Quiriguá</a> in 738</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Copán is an archaeological site, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1980, 1.6 kilometres from the village of Copán Ruinas.</span></div>
        </div>
