    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Copán was occupied from the Early Preclassic period to the Postclassic, and in 426 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-yax-kuk-mo">K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'</a> refounded it as the capital of a new Maya kingdom whose dynasty ruled for four centuries.</li><li>Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waxaklajuun-ubaah-kawiil">Uaxaclajuun Ub'aah K'awiil</a> raised the city to its height and ranked it among the four most powerful Maya states, but he was captured and beheaded by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quirigua">Quiriguá</a> in 738.</li><li>The dynasty ended in the early 9th century amid overpopulation and disease, the ceremonial centre was abandoned by the early 10th century, and archaeologists have worked there since the 19th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Hieroglyphic Stairway of Copán takes its name from some 2,200 glyphs that together form the longest known Maya hieroglyphic text, which is still being reconstructed because the glyphic blocks were scrambled when the temple's façade collapsed.</p></aside>
    </section>

